Today's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a> update addresses over 100 security vulnerabilities, which is a good reason to install the new software.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/apple-lock-security-bug-vulnerability-fix-privacy.jpg" alt="" width="2016" height="1134" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1038581" /><br/>
If you don't want to update to &zwnj;iOS 27&zwnj; yet, Apple also released iOS 26.7 with over 80 security fixes. 75 of the fixes are shared across both updates, so iOS 26.7 addresses many of the same issues.<br/>
<br/>
There are multiple kernel fixes, including one that could allow a malicious app to gain root access and another that could allow for Bluetooth remote code execution. A full list of vulnerabilities can be found on Apple's site for both <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/149034">iOS 27</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/149041">iOS 26.7</a>.<br/>
<br/>
None of the flaws are known to have been actively exploited, but now that details have been published, devices that are not updated could be vulnerable.<br/>
<br/>
